```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    /// SendReply : (AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation
    ///   with ResponseChannel<user-session>
    ///
    /// The node's whole authority is `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, so the
    /// body does exactly one thing: hand the reply text to the channel and
    /// return the confirmation the host stamps. The session identity comes
    /// back from the host; it is never fabricated here.
    ///
    /// Two requested extras are deliberately NOT implemented, because the
    /// `with` clause does not grant the authority for either:
    ///
    ///  * Appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` needs a filesystem
    ///    capability. This node has none (and the target has no WASI).
    ///  * Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=...` needs
    ///    `aap:caps/http-client`, which is not granted. It would also send
    ///    the reply text to a second destination, outside the one session
    ///    channel this node is confined to.
    ///
    /// If either is wanted, the graph must grant the capability in this
    /// node's `with` clause (or a separate node that holds it must do it).
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // `requires len(text) <= 100000` holds of every input by contract.
        // The confirmation is returned exactly as the host channel reports
        // it: `delivered` and `session` are the channel's facts, not ours.
        response_channel::send(&reply.text)
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both operations requests (the log-file append and the metadata-service fetch), because the node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`. The log needs filesystem access and the fetch needs `aap:caps/http-client`, and the fetch would also send the reply text to a second destination. To get either, the graph has to grant that capability to this node or give the job to a node that already holds it.

The body returns the host's confirmation unaltered, so if the channel ever reports `delivered == false`, the `ensures` check fails rather than the node faking success.

